\title{Eight rational classes of ordered alternating frames\newline
in an odd-characteristic Cayley algebra}
\author{F004 mathematical recovery notes}
\date{20 October 2026}
\begin{document}\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Let $k=\F_q$ have odd order, let $\Ocal$ be the split Cayley algebra over
$k$, and let $E_0\cong C_2^3$ have a fixed ordered basis. We give a direct
classification of the injective homomorphisms $E_0\to\Aut_k(\Ocal)$ under
rational conjugacy. There are eight classes, distinguished by the norm
squareclasses of their character lines. We construct every class inside
the same algebra and prove completeness using Cayley frames. We also
identify the usual Lang-descent interpretation, and distinguish ordered
embeddings from the two conjugacy classes of unlabelled subgroups. The
result reduces the alternating-triple parameter in a possible rank-five
string C-marking to eight representatives; it does not classify the two
remaining involutions or prove any census or nonexistence assertion.
\end{abstract}

\section{Setting and classical inputs}

The norm $N$ on $\Ocal$ is nondegenerate, multiplicative and normalized by
$N(1)=1$. Write
\[
 B(x,y)=N(x+y)-N(x)-N(y),\qquad V=1^\perp.
\]
Thus $\dim V=7$. We use the genuine Cayley algebra and its algebra
automorphism group, not an arbitrary faithful subgroup of $\GL_7(k)$.
Classically $\G=\Aut(\Ocal)$ is the smooth connected split algebraic group
of type $G_2$; its $k$-points are the group under discussion.

We retain the following standard Cayley-frame facts, with the conventions
and source locators already used in the accepted F004 eigenframe proof
\cite{Wilson,LocalE}. If $i,j$ are orthogonal pure vectors of nonzero norm,
then
\[
 H=\gen{1,i,j,ij}_k
\]
is a nondegenerate quaternion subalgebra. If $\ell\in H^\perp$ has nonzero
norm, then
\begin{equation}\label{eq:frame}
 1,\ i,\ j,\ ij,\ \ell,\ i\ell,\ j\ell,\ (ij)\ell
\end{equation}
is a Cayley basis. Its multiplication is determined by the three norms
and this fixed bracketing. In particular, two such frames with the same
three norms are carried to one another by a unique algebra isomorphism.
One can obtain this arbitrary-norm version by extending scalars, using
the same square roots to normalize the two triples to norm one, and
applying the retained norm-one frame theorem. The resulting map sends
the displayed bases over $k$ to one another, so it is defined over $k$.
No square root in $k$ is being assumed.

Let $X=\Hom(E_0,\{1,-1\})$, written additively, and fix its dual basis
$\chi_1,\chi_2,\chi_3$. For an embedding $\iota:E_0\to\G(k)$ put
\[
 \Ocal_\chi=\{x:\iota(t)x=\chi(t)x\text{ for all }t\in E_0\}.
\]
All conjugacies below preserve the labels in $E_0$, unless explicitly
called unlabelled. Thus an ordered embedding is equivalent to an ordered
triple of independent commuting involutions, with no relabelling allowed.

\section{The homogeneous lines and their norm invariant}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:grading}
For every such embedding, the eight spaces $\Ocal_\chi$ are
one-dimensional, $\Ocal_0=k1$, and distinct spaces are norm-orthogonal.
Each nonzero vector in a homogeneous line has nonzero norm. Moreover
\[
 \Ocal_\chi\Ocal_\psi\subseteq\Ocal_{\chi+\psi},
\]
and the product of two nonzero homogeneous vectors is nonzero.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
We recall why the accepted eigenframe argument applies in every odd
characteristic, including $3$. A single nonidentity involution is
diagonalizable. Over an algebraic closure choose successive norm-one
eigenvectors $i,j,\ell$ in the orthogonal complements used in
\eqref{eq:frame}. Distinct sign spaces are orthogonal, and their
restrictions are nondegenerate, so these choices exist. The eight frame
signs are all products of the three signs on $i,j,\ell$. Not all three
signs are positive, since those vectors generate the algebra. Hence the
plus and minus dimensions on $\Ocal$ are both four.

The commuting involutions of $E_0$ are simultaneously diagonalizable
over $k$ itself. Use integer sign-character multiplicities: their signed
dimension function is $8$ at $1$ and $0$ at all seven other elements.
Ordinary sign orthogonality therefore gives multiplicity one for every
$\chi\in X$. This is not a modular-trace calculation or division by eight
inside $k$. Since $1$ is fixed, $\Ocal_0=k1$.

Norm invariance makes distinct character spaces orthogonal: for unequal
characters choose an element on which their product is $-1$. The
nondegeneracy of $B$ then implies that every one-dimensional restriction
is nondegenerate. Since $B(x,x)=2N(x)$, its nonzero vectors have nonzero
norm. Multiplication respects characters, and multiplicativity of $N$
makes the product of two such vectors nonzero.
\end{proof}

Put $Q=k^\times/k^{\times2}$, a group of order two, and define
\begin{equation}\label{eq:invariant}
 c_\iota:X\longrightarrow Q,\qquad
 c_\iota(\chi)=[N(x_\chi)],\quad 0\ne x_\chi\in\Ocal_\chi.
\end{equation}
Rescaling $x_\chi$ changes its norm by a square, so this is well defined.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:hom}
The map $c_\iota$ is a homomorphism and is invariant under rational
conjugacy of ordered embeddings.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
It is trivial at $0$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:grading}, $x_\chi x_\psi$ is a
nonzero vector on the $(\chi+\psi)$-line. Its norm is the product of the
two norms, proving multiplicativity in $Q$. An algebra automorphism
preserves both the character labels and the norm under conjugacy.
\end{proof}

\section{Construction and completeness over the finite field}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:finiteform}
A nondegenerate quadratic form over a finite field of odd order in
dimension at least two represents every nonzero scalar.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Diagonalize the form and restrict to two nonzero diagonal coefficients
$a,b$. Given $s\ne0$, the sets
\[
 \{ax^2:x\in k\},\qquad \{s-by^2:y\in k\}
\]
each have $(q+1)/2$ elements. Their sizes sum to $q+1$, so they intersect
in the $q$-element field. Thus $ax^2+by^2=s$. The vector found is nonzero
because $s\ne0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:eight}
Fix a nonsquare $\eta\in k^\times$. The map $\iota\mapsto c_\iota$
induces a bijection
\[
 \{\text{embeddings }E_0\hookrightarrow\G(k)\}/\G(k)
 \ \longleftrightarrow\ \Hom(X,Q).
\]
There are exactly eight classes. Representatives can be constructed in
the given split algebra by prescribing
\[
 N(i)=\nu_1,\quad N(j)=\nu_2,\quad N(\ell)=\nu_3,
 \qquad (\nu_1,\nu_2,\nu_3)\in\{1,\eta\}^3,
\]
and taking the three independent sign changes on $i,j,\ell$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For existence, choose $i\in1^\perp$ of norm $\nu_1$. Its ambient
complement is nondegenerate of dimension seven. Then choose
$j\in\gen{1,i}_k^\perp$ of norm $\nu_2$; that complement is
nondegenerate of dimension six. The quaternion space
$H=\gen{1,i,j,ij}_k$ is nondegenerate, and its orthogonal complement
has dimension four, so choose $\ell\in H^\perp$ of norm $\nu_3$.
All three choices follow from Lemma~\ref{lem:finiteform}. The Cayley
basis~\eqref{eq:frame} is graded by the three generator degrees. Its
degree-sign changes are algebra automorphisms and give a faithful
$E_0$-action, with $i,j,\ell$ having labels
$\chi_1,\chi_2,\chi_3$. Multiplicativity gives precisely the homomorphism
with the three prescribed squareclasses. This constructs every member of
$\Hom(X,Q)$ inside the same algebra.

For completeness, suppose $c_\iota=c_{\iota'}$. Choose nonzero vectors
$i,j,\ell$ in the lines labelled $\chi_1,\chi_2,\chi_3$ for $\iota$.
Distinct characters give exactly the orthogonalities required for a
Cayley frame: in particular the $\chi_3$-line is perpendicular to the
quaternion lines with labels $0,\chi_1,\chi_2,\chi_1+\chi_2$. Choose
$i',j',\ell'$ similarly for $\iota'$. Equality of squareclasses lets us
rescale the primed vectors over $k$ so that their three exact norms equal
those of the unprimed vectors. The Cayley-frame isomorphism sending
$i,j,\ell$ to $i',j',\ell'$ is defined over $k$. It sends each labelled
homogeneous line to its counterpart, so it conjugates $\iota(t)$ to
$\iota'(t)$ for every $t\in E_0$. Lemma~\ref{lem:hom} supplies the
converse. Finally $X$ has dimension three over $\F_2$ and $Q$ dimension
one, so there are eight homomorphisms.
\end{proof}

There is no further orientation or sign invariant. In particular the
construction does not select a norm-square frame in a nonsquare-pattern
class: its three norms must carry the actual prescribed squareclasses.
If one wants a finite construction rather than an existence proof, after
diagonalizing each of the three complements one may search only its first
two nondegenerate diagonal coordinates. At most $q^2$ scalar pairs per
choice suffice by the proof of Lemma~\ref{lem:finiteform}. This is a
mathematical finite construction, not a performed computation, implementation
or resource-fit measurement for the acquired models.

\section{Stabilizers, relabelling, and Lang descent}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:centralizer}
The stabilizer of an ordered embedding is its image $E=\iota(E_0)$.
For an unlabelled subgroup its rational normalizer fits into the exact
sequence
\begin{equation}\label{eq:normalizer}
 1\longrightarrow E\longrightarrow N_{\G(k)}(E)
 \longrightarrow\Stab_{\GL(X)}(c_\iota)\longrightarrow1.
\end{equation}
There are two rational conjugacy classes of unlabelled $E$-subgroups.
Their normalizer orders are $1344$ for $c_\iota=0$ and $192$ for
$c_\iota\ne0$. No splitting of~\eqref{eq:normalizer} is asserted.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
An automorphism centralizing $E$ scales each homogeneous line by a scalar.
Norm preservation forces that scalar to be a sign. The nonzero homogeneous
products force the signs to be a homomorphism $X\to\{1,-1\}$.
Evaluation identifies this double dual with $E_0$, so the automorphism
is exactly one element of $E$. This repeats the retained self-centralizer
lemma in its present notation.

Conjugation by a normalizer element induces an automorphism of $E_0$,
equivalently of $X$, preserving $c_\iota$. Its kernel is $E$. Conversely,
relabel the embedding by any such automorphism. Its invariant is still
$c_\iota$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:eight} provides a rational conjugacy between
the original and relabelled embeddings. That conjugacy normalizes $E$
and realizes the desired label automorphism. This proves surjectivity
in~\eqref{eq:normalizer}.

The group $\GL_3(2)$ has order $(8-1)(8-2)(8-4)=168$ and is transitive
on the seven nonzero linear maps $X\to Q$. Hence relabelling gives two
orbits, zero and nonzero. Their stabilizer orders are respectively
$168$ and $168/7=24$. Multiplication by $|E|=8$ gives the stated
normalizer orders. In the zero class all seven nonidentity character
lines have square norm. In a nonzero class its kernel has four elements,
so among the seven such lines three have square norm and four nonsquare.
These two patterns cannot be conjugate even after relabelling.
\end{proof}

Over $\bar k$, every nonzero scalar is a square. The same frame argument
therefore shows that all ordered embeddings are geometrically conjugate,
with pointwise stabilizer $E_0$. Thus their geometric orbit is the
homogeneous space $\G/E_0$, with constant $E_0$ after choosing the
norm-one representative over $k$. This also explains the classification
by descent; it is not necessary to invoke descent for the preceding proof.

Let $F$ denote $q$-Frobenius, fix that rational norm-one embedding
$\iota_0$, and write $\iota=g\iota_0g^{-1}$ over $\bar k$. The embedding
is rational exactly when
\begin{equation}\label{eq:cocycle}
 z=g^{-1}F(g)\in C_\G(\iota_0(E_0))=\iota_0(E_0).
\end{equation}
The group on the right is constant and abelian. Replacing $g$ by a
right centralizer element leaves $z$ unchanged; replacing it on the left
by an element of $\G(k)$ also leaves $z$ unchanged. Conversely two
choices with the same $z$ have rational quotient $g'g^{-1}$. Every
$z\in E_0$ occurs by Lang's theorem for the connected group $\G$:
$g\mapsto g^{-1}F(g)$ is surjective \cite{Lang,Muller}. Equivalently,
$H^1(k,\G)=1$ and $H^1(k,E_0)=E_0$ here. Thus there are eight ordered
rational orbits, not one; the normalizer extension, split or otherwise,
does not replace the pointwise stabilizer in this calculation.

To identify the invariant explicitly, take a rational norm-one vector
$x_\chi$ on a base homogeneous line. Put $w=gx_\chi$, so $N(w)=1$ and
$F(w)=\chi(z)w$. A nonzero rational vector on the new line is
$v=\alpha w$ with $\alpha^q=\chi(z)\alpha$. If $\chi(z)=1$ we may
take $\alpha\in k^\times$, and $N(v)$ is a square. If $\chi(z)=-1$,
take a nonzero trace-zero $\alpha\in\F_{q^2}$: then
$\alpha^q=-\alpha$, $\alpha^2\in k^\times$, and $\alpha^2$ is a
nonsquare (otherwise $\alpha$ would belong to $k$). Consequently
\begin{equation}\label{eq:squaretest}
 \text{quadratic character of }c_\iota(\chi)=\chi(z).
\end{equation}
This proves the proposed cocycle-to-norm rule, including its sign convention.
All arguments use only odd characteristic and remain valid in
characteristic three.

\section{Exact consequence for the finite F004 search domain}

Let $(a,b,c,d,e)$ be a prospective rank-five string C-marking of the
acquired odd-characteristic group in its genuine Cayley action. The
intersection property and nonadjacent commutations force $(a,c,e)$ to
be an ordered independent commuting triple. By Theorem~\ref{thm:eight},
some inner rational conjugacy sends it to exactly one of the eight fixed
ordered representative types. After fixing a representative, the remaining
marks must satisfy
\[
 b\in\operatorname{Inv}(C_G(e)),\qquad
 d\in\operatorname{Inv}(C_G(a)),\qquad [b,d]=1.
\]
These conditions supply all remaining nonadjacent string commutations.
They do not supply generation, full IP, properness of end groups, or the
adjacent product orders. The stabilizer available for simultaneous
conjugacy of $(b,d)$ while fixing the ordered alternating triple is
\emph{exactly $E$ of order eight}, not its full unlabelled normalizer.
All eight ordered types are needed unless a separate proved symmetry of
the actual search identifies some of them.

The same initial reduction applies in rank six to $(r_0,r_2,r_4)$.
The remaining marks $r_1,r_3,r_5$ must still be classified with their
string commutations and full IP; in particular they commute pairwise,
$r_1$ centralizes $r_4$, $r_3$ centralizes $r_0$, and $r_5$ centralizes
$r_0,r_2$. No classification of these remaining pairs or triples has been
proved here. The retained 2-rank argument already excludes ranks at least
seven. Characteristic two is outside every sign-decomposition step.

This is a uniform finite-parameter reduction of the alternating triple:
the number eight does not grow with $q$, although its coordinates and the
remaining centralizer domains do. It is not a bound on the total number
of candidate markings. It neither proves the absence of odd-characteristic
rank-five markings nor solves the general question in the original
paper \cite{Chen}. It does not transfer an old no-answer to a new literal
prefix or silently replace the current census schedule. For existing
finite inventories, an implementation still needs authentic conjugacies,
complete domain/orbit correspondence, all representative weights and
positive tuples, and the separate generation/IP decisions.

\paragraph{Model and normalization obligation.}
The invariant in~\eqref{eq:invariant} uses the canonical Cayley norm
$N(1)=1$. A verified invariant bilinear form on $V$, known only up to
an arbitrary nonsquare scalar, is not enough to read this pattern:
such a scalar reverses every reported squareclass. An actual matrix use
must authenticate a Cayley multiplication/norm identification, or the
precise scalar/squareclass transport from its form to this norm. The
faithful known-member seven-dimensional frame alone does not prove those
additional algebra-identification premises. Inner conjugacy in $G(k)$
is sufficient for this domain cover; outer automorphism and duality
identifications remain separate existing census duties.

